RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Non-Carcinogenic Health Risks
The findings in Table 3 showed that polluted
water sources in Gombe LGA posed a
serious non-carcinogenic health risk to all
age groups, with tap, sachet, and well water
being the most problematic. The drop in
CDI readings from adults to children to
infants is probably caused by younger age
groups using less water rather than by lower
levels of pollution.

These high CDI values are especially
alarming for children since they are typically
more susceptible to toxicants. However,
while the trend remains the same, infants
have lower overall CDI levels than both
adults and children. Well water (0.554275)
and sachet water (0.493652) have the
highest CDI levels, while tap water
(0.380725) comes in second. The most
important factor influencing high CDI
readings in all age groups and water sources
is chromium (Cr). This implies that the
water supply in Gombe may be seriously
contaminated by Cr.

The highest CDI readings for adults are
found in well water (1.080039) and sachet
water (0.961911). The main causes of these
elevated levels are Cr and Pb. A
comparatively high CDI (0.741865) is also

